## 1.4 Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) & The Evolution of KV-Cache Compression

To understand why prompt caching has become technically feasible at hyperscale across billions of tokens, we must analyze the architectural transition from Multi-Head Attention (MHA) to **Grouped-Query Attention (GQA)** in modern foundation models.

### From Multi-Head to Grouped-Query Attention
In first-generation large language models (such as GPT-3), standard Multi-Head Attention instantiated an equal number of Query ($Q$), Key ($K$), and Value ($V$) heads:

$$H_Q = H_K = H_V = H$$

If a model operated with 64 attention heads ($H = 64$), the GPU was required to maintain 64 independent Key matrices and 64 Value matrices in HBM memory for every single token in the context window. As sequence lengths expanded from 4,000 tokens to 200,000 tokens, the memory bandwidth required to fetch these massive matrices during autoregressive decoding completely saturated memory channels, inducing severe memory bus thrashing and driving inference costs to unsustainable levels.

To solve this hardware bottleneck, modern foundation models (including Claude 3.5 Sonnet) adopt **Grouped-Query Attention (GQA)**. 

Under GQA, the query heads are partitioned into $G$ groups, with each group sharing a single Key and Value head:

$$\text{Ratio} = \frac{H_Q}{H_{KV}} = \gamma$$

Where $\gamma$ is typically set to 4 or 8.

When $\gamma = 8$, 8 distinct query heads share a single key-value projection. The corresponding physical memory footprint of the KV-cache is reduced by an immediate factor of 8:

$$\text{Memory}_{\text{KV-GQA}} = \frac{\text{Memory}_{\text{KV-MHA}}}{\gamma} = \frac{52.4 \text{ GB}}{8} \approx \mathbf{6.55 \text{ Gigabytes}}$$

This dramatic 87.5% hardware memory reduction is the precise technological breakthrough that enables Anthropic to maintain persistent 5-minute KV-caches across millions of concurrent enterprise sessions in GPU HBM without exhausting physical datacenter memory.

