## 2.4 Managing Cache Eviction Under High-Concurrency Enterprise Loads

While Anthropic specifies a rolling 5-minute Time-To-Live (TTL) for cached prompts, enterprise architects operating high-concurrency systems must understand the internal operational dynamics of **GPU Memory Eviction Policies**.

### The LRU Cache Eviction Mechanism
In high-demand cloud inference clusters, GPU High-Bandwidth Memory is a finite physical resource. When sudden bursts of global user activity saturate available HBM capacity on active worker nodes, the inference orchestration layer executes **Least Recently Used (LRU) Cache Eviction**:
* Sessions with the longest elapsed idle times since their previous token generation are evicted from HBM first.
* Even if an enterprise session has only been idle for 3 minutes and 40 seconds (well within the theoretical 5-minute TTL window), intense cluster-wide memory contention can trigger premature cache eviction.
* When the agent dispatches its subsequent turn, it experiences an unexpected **Cold-Start Cache Miss**, incurring the 125% cache write fee ($3.75/M) and experiencing a 10x latency spike during the prefill phase.

### Engineering Mitigations for Premature Eviction
To protect mission-critical autonomous workflows from LRU cache eviction:
1. **Shorten Heartbeat Intervals Under High Load**:
   During peak corporate business hours (e.g., 09:00 to 17:00 EST), reduce the keep-alive ping interval from 270 seconds down to **180 seconds** (3 minutes). This frequent activity signals high operational priority to the inference gateway, dramatically lowering the probability of premature LRU eviction.
2. **Session Affinity & Sticky Routing**:
   If your enterprise routes API calls through an internal proxy across multiple Anthropic API keys or AWS Bedrock regions, enforce **Session Affinity**. All turns belonging to a specific agent session must route to the exact same inference cluster endpoint. If Turn 1 is processed in AWS `us-east-1` and Turn 2 is routed to `us-west-2`, the second region will have no record of the KV-cache, resulting in a 100% cache miss.

